# Case study and portfolio writing

Write like a professional designer explaining work to a hiring manager who has not lived the project. Complete sentences. Warm, not cute. Specific, not long.

This rule outranks the default “be concise” habit when the output is case copy, page copy, or a document the user will read. Concise means one idea per paragraph, not deleted verbs, articles, or context.

## Voice

- Full sentences with a subject and a verb. If it would look like a ticket title or a code comment, rewrite it.
- 2–4 sentences per block. One idea per paragraph. Not a fragment, not an essay.
- First person (`I` / `we`) is fine. Do not hide behind “the team executed research.”
- Open with the human situation, then the work. The reader needs the world before the process.
- Connect cause to choice: “Because users still thought there was only one ancestor, we…”
- If a term, metric, or tool is not obvious, say what it is in the next clause.
- US English. Professional-warm. Light personality is good; speech-to-text rambling is not.
- Captions are sentences, not labels.

## Headers

A skimmer who reads only the header should know what the section is about.

Use four parents, each shown once: Problem, Process, Solution, and What's next. On a shipped case with measured results, the last parent is Results.

A subsection title names what is in the paragraphs under it, in plain language: “The quadrant chart”, “What the manager cannot see”. It is not a pull quote, a fragment, or an internal crumb (`Rough`, `Evidence`, `One chart for the four groups`).

Role / year / team can be a short facts strip. Story sections cannot.

Every rich case uses these parents. Do not bring back Rough, Evidence, or Making.

## Decisions and findings

- A constraint from the brief is context, not a decision the designer made.
- When several people were interviewed, give each person one scannable bullet. A longer problem for one person can stay in prose.
- A caption is one sentence and sits in the text column. Under a prototype, that sentence is the only caption. “Open in a new tab” stays the link.
- Regroup existing paragraphs. Do not write a new essay to fill a new header.

## Do not write like this

- Fragments: “Card sort. One ancestor at a time. Shipped July 24.”
- Slash-stacks: “research + production data + usability”
- Cute headers that hide the content: “One chart for the four groups”
- Parents with no subsection that says what is below
- Padding: restating the same point three ways, or a page of explanation for one decision

## Write like this

> The Pioneers were a group of tenacious individuals who left everything they knew to follow what they believed and settle the west.

> Approximately one out of four users has unintentionally revealed information they wished to keep private while lending their phones to friends and family.

> Based on user feedback we decided that we needed to do a better job helping our users feel like we were giving them interesting ancestors to discover instead of a list of tasks to complete.

> Problem
> What the manager cannot see

> Ellis, a front-line manager, can see that Tara is Developing and still cannot tell a bad-fit book from a weak skill, or which skill is worth his one hour.

References for tone (do not clone structure blindly): Morten Winther parking case, Dousan Miao Private Space, Cody’s FamilySearch Behance case studies. Sean Halpin’s AI case is a visual reference, not a heading reference. Caliber (`caliber-skill-on-deals`) is the structure to follow. Do not treat the rest of the live portfolio as a copy model.
